window.MirchiSearch=[{"title": "Business & leaders · How a station earns repeat advertising revenue", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#booking", "text": "How a station earns repeat advertising revenue Agree the brief The buyer selects a city, time slot and outcome. Deliver and verify Production records broadcasts and checks any missed spots. Review and renew Sales and the buyer review delivery, response and the next booking."}, {"title": "Business & leaders · Audience and buyer", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#audience", "text": "Audience and buyer The listener receives the programme for free. The advertiser pays to reach listeners in a selected city and time slot. For a café, an offer code can link some store visits to an ad. The station can confirm delivery using broadcast logs."}, {"title": "Business & leaders · Campaign fees and event income", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#commercial-uses", "text": "Campaign fees and event income A café can buy a host-led tasting as well as airtime. The fee covers agreed work. Venue, staffing and production costs reduce the money the station keeps. A ticketed event can also earn admission and sponsor income according to the organiser’s agreement."}, {"title": "Business & leaders · Client objectives and campaign delivery", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#client-examples", "text": "Client objectives and campaign delivery A car, a home appliance, a tin of paint and an insurance policy are bought in different ways. That is why these four companies used Mirchi differently. ENIL, the company that operates Radio Mirchi, described the work in its report for April 2024 to March 2025. MG Hector took the radio host to the showroom. Think about buying a car. An ad can help you remember the name, but you will probably want to sit in the car and drive it. Mirchi hosts took part in showroom test drives and talked about the car’s features with participants. For the showroom manager, the next question is simple: how many people who tried the car came back to discuss buying one? Xiaomi took its products to people’s homes. Instead of waiting for families to visit an electronics store, its Smart Home League brought demonstrations and challenges to ten residential communities. Residents could see the products in use. Posts about the activity were displayed more than a million times, according to ENIL. That tells Xiaomi how widely the posts appeared. To learn what residents bought afterwards, it would also need sales records. Dulux made the paint shops the centre of the story. Dulux sells through dealers: shop owners who stock its paint. It ran a display contest involving 20 selected dealers across five cities. Mirchi hosts visited shops, and entries were featured on radio and online. The people Dulux wanted to involve first were the shop owners themselves. A shop could earn recognition for a good display, and Dulux could give its products a more prominent place in the store. ACKO met people at a run. Insurance is usually something people think about when they need a policy. ACKO also appeared in neighbourhood activities and a Neon Run across five cities. The run gave people an enjoyable occasion to encounter the brand. A runner attending, remembering ACKO and later buying a policy are three different outcomes. The report describes the event and leaves the sales outcome open. These are examples of work delivered. The report gives no fee or profit for each client. ENIL FY25 annual report, pages 15 and 16 One number in plain English. For July to September 2026, ENIL said radio generated 53% of its Indian revenue. Imagine all the money its Indian businesses earned in that quarter as ₹100. About ₹53 came from radio. The figure describes revenue before costs. S2"}, {"title": "Business & leaders · How are ENIL, Mirchi and Gaana connected?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#company", "text": "How are ENIL, Mirchi and Gaana connected? ENIL is the company. Mirchi is its radio and entertainment brand. Gaana is its music streaming service. ENIL stands for Entertainment Network (India) Limited. It belongs to the wider Times Group and publishes its own financial results. Group revenue figures belong to ENIL and carry a reporting period. S6 S2 Who pays whom? Imagine a Delhi café buying an ad on a Mirchi morning show. The café pays to reach listeners. A Gaana subscriber pays to listen to music. Both activities sit within ENIL’s business, but they earn money in different ways. S7 S5 Where do Mirchi Plus and Mirchi Live fit? Mirchi Plus is a name readers may see on digital content and app listings. Mirchi Live is where people can find events and campaigns such as Mirchi Freshers. S20 S12"}, {"title": "Business & leaders · ENIL leadership and group responsibilities", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#leaders", "text": "ENIL leadership and group responsibilities View company leadership and responsibilities Leader Role in latest presentation Business responsibilities to clarify Yatish Mehrishi Chief Executive Officer Portfolio choices, profitable growth, capital discipline. Preeti Nihalani Chief Operating Officer City execution, pricing, sales enablement and cross-functional delivery. Sumit Aggrawal Chief Strategy & Growth Officer Growth opportunities, partnerships and scalable operating models. Udit Tyagi Chief Digital Officer Digital product, technology and experimentation. Sanjay Ballabh Chief Financial Officer Margins, collections, revenue and costs for each user group and investment trade-offs. Titles above follow the Q2 FY27 presentation; discussion themes are my analytical topics, not stated individual mandates. S1 S8"}, {"title": "Business & leaders · What is difficult to reproduce", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#assets", "text": "What is difficult to reproduce Analysis Local relevance A useful city personality understands language, humour, neighbourhoods and cultural timing. Treat that relationship as a product asset, with editorial judgment and talent continuity. Analysis Advertiser relationships A local sales relationship can open doors for several formats. The product challenge is making offers easy to understand, deliver and measure without fragmenting ownership. Analysis IP and execution A recognisable format can travel across cities, but execution depends on local demand, venue economics and sponsor fit. A repeatable playbook should preserve local relevance."}, {"title": "Business & leaders · How central teams and city teams could work together", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#operations", "text": "How central teams and city teams could work together Central: product platform, analytics definitions, rights standards, billing rules, experimentation and reusable creative tooling. City: programming, local creators, advertiser relationships, event partners and cultural adaptation. Shared governance: deal approval, sponsorship fulfilment, audience permissions and profit after direct costs reporting. Who can make each decision: ask which team manages the account, product budget, pricing, agreed delivery standard and how revenue is allocated across teams. This is a proposed working model for discovery. It does not claim to describe ENIL’s current reporting lines."}, {"title": "Business & leaders · International: a local diaspora business", "url": "/radio-mirchi/business#international", "text": "International: a local diaspora business ENIL’s public profile describes a presence in the USA, UAE, Qatar and Bahrain. Its FY26 operating review discusses diaspora engagement and partnership / licensing opportunities. These are company disclosures, not a fresh station-by-station international audit. S7 S5 Commercial lever Question to investigate Diaspora relevance Which language, music and community formats create a repeat habit in each market? Local advertiser demand What customer categories pay for access, and how concentrated are the accounts? Operating structure Which markets are owned, operated with partners, franchised or licensed, and who bears the cost? Rights and distribution Which content can cross borders, and on which digital or broadcast platforms? Capital discipline What local profit after direct costs, cash commitment and partner performance justify expansion? International growth should be evaluated market by market. A recognisable Indian brand can help acquisition, but local demand, rights and execution still determine the economics."}, {"title": "How Mirchi earns · Follow one radio booking", "url": "/radio-mirchi/revenue#diagram-1", "text": "Follow one radio booking 30 broadcasts A café orders a message that lasts 20 seconds. 600 ad seconds 30 broadcasts × 20 seconds each. ₹30,000 in revenue At an example price of ₹50 per second, before costs. Illustration only. Actual prices depend on the station, time and agreement."}, {"title": "How Mirchi earns · How the money is made", "url": "/radio-mirchi/revenue#streams", "text": "How the money is made Engine Buyer / value purchased Revenue logic Costs and constraints Paid radio ad time Advertiser buys scheduled exposure to a city audience. Sold ad seconds × actual actual price earned. Finite inventory, discounts, agency terms, seasonality, programming and station costs. Integrated brand solutions Brand buys an idea executed across RJ content, on-ground and digital touchpoints. Project fee, sponsorship package or contracted deliverables. Production, talent, vendors, revisions and delivery complexity. Events & IP Sponsors, ticket buyers and commercial partners. Sponsorship + net ticket receipts + contracted ancillary income. Artists, venue, production, permits, security, marketing, refunds and cash advances. Digital solutions Advertiser buys content, creators, inventory or managed campaign work. Campaign/service fee; recognise revenue according to contract and accounting policy. Creator fees, paid media, production, platform dependence; billing that includes money passed on to partners can inflate gross revenue. Gaana Listener or distribution partner buys premium access. Subscription / partnership consideration over time covered by the plan. Rights, payment/channel fees, support, tech, acquisition and retention. International Local advertisers, partners and diaspora audiences. Market-specific operations, partnerships or licensing structures. Local regulation, rights, currency and partner economics. Business categories are grounded in ENIL disclosures; the formulas describe commercial mechanics and are analytical, not published ENIL rate cards. S7 S5"}, {"title": "How Mirchi earns · The radio equation", "url": "/radio-mirchi/revenue#radio-math", "text": "The radio equation Model Net Revenue from paid radio ad time = ad seconds available × percentage of ad time sold × actual revenue per second. Use the same city, time of day and time period for every input. A 5% price reduction needs more than 5% additional sold volume to keep revenue unchanged: 1 ÷ 0.95 − 1 = 5.26%. If capacity or listener ad tolerance is already constrained, the extra volume may not be achievable. Discounting should be evaluated against total profit after direct costs, advertiser retention and future pricing expectations. Percentage of ad time sold and price are not independent. Prime time, local festivals, premium sponsorships and bundled contracts alter the inventory mix. A national average can hide both healthy cities and weak stations."}, {"title": "How Mirchi earns · From advertiser brief to repeat budget", "url": "/radio-mirchi/revenue#sales", "text": "From advertiser brief to repeat budget Step Product / process opportunity Commercial measure Define objective One city, one audience, one business outcome, one budget range. Qualified brief to proposal rate. Package offer Standard modules with explicit deliverables and optional custom work. Proposal time and profit after delivery costs at quote. Approve & collect Margin review, advance collection and cancellation terms. Cash collected before committed vendor spend. Execute One delivery plan linking radio logs, creator assets and event tasks. On-time fulfilment and rework cost. Report & renew Show delivered media separately from response and causal lift. Repeat booking and profit from renewals. Recommended product principle: make the next purchase easier without promising giving credit for a result precision that the campaign design cannot support."}, {"title": "How Mirchi earns · Questions that prevent misleading revenue stories", "url": "/radio-mirchi/revenue#accounting", "text": "Questions that prevent misleading revenue stories Is ticket revenue shown gross or net of platform fees, taxes and organiser share? Is an event owned, co-produced, commissioned or only promoted? Branding alone does not establish IP ownership. Do digital solutions include media purchased on behalf of clients? Separate billings from retained margin. When is sponsorship cash collected versus recorded as revenue? Does a bundle pay Gaana on shipment, activation or usage? What is the effective net consideration? Are sold to existing customers revenues extra or shifted from another Mirchi budget?"}, {"title": "Radio & advertisers · What radio does well", "url": "/radio-mirchi/radio#role", "text": "What radio does well A useful radio proposition combines music with familiar personalities, local context, timely information and participation. The commercial product is access to that relationship. A listener who enjoys an RJ is not automatically a digital user or a ticket buyer; every handoff still has to earn the next action. The corporate profile lists 73 stations in 63 cities, with the listener figure on that page explicitly dated to IRS 2019. Use it to understand historical scale, not current unique reach. S7"}, {"title": "Radio & advertisers · A senior PM’s radio dashboard", "url": "/radio-mirchi/radio#inventory", "text": "A senior PM’s radio dashboard Question Metric to inspect Decision Where is value leaking? City × station × time of day actual price earned, percentage of ad time sold and profit after direct costs. Price, bundle or inventory allocation. Which advertisers repeat? User group repeat spend, category, account size and collections. Strengthen the proposition for valuable segments. Are campaigns delivered? Booked versus aired spots, make-goods and exceptions. Fix delivery reliability before expanding promises. What does the audience tolerate? Ad minutes, tune-out signals where measurable, complaints and listener research. Protect experience while selling inventory. Does a cross-channel campaign work? Tagged response and an agreed extra results caused by the change design. Prove impact at the appropriate confidence level. These are metrics to request internally. No internal dashboard or listening panel was available for this study."}, {"title": "Radio & advertisers · Proposed pilot: a local advertiser return loop", "url": "/radio-mirchi/radio#pilot", "text": "Proposed pilot: a local advertiser return loop Analysis Customer need A local business needs a credible way to bring relevant people to a store or offer, and an easy way to decide whether to buy another campaign. It may have neither a marketing analyst nor a full agency. Analysis Minimum product Create a scoped campaign brief, a verified landing page or call response mechanism, a delivery log and a concise close-out report. Sell a bounded test before automating the entire sales process. Analysis Validation Select a small, comparable advertiser user group; predefine one primary outcome. Use matched stores or time/geographic comparison groups that do not receive the change when practical. Report uncertainty, not just attributed clicks. Success criterion to agree before launch: higher extra profit after direct costs from repeat advertisers with acceptable collection days, no rise in delivery exceptions and no deterioration in listener experience."}, {"title": "Radio & advertisers · Rights and regulatory dependencies", "url": "/radio-mirchi/radio#constraints", "text": "Rights and regulatory dependencies FM broadcast, online streaming, podcasts, social clips and event music can have different rights and contract conditions. Obtain a rights review for each intended reuse rather than treating a broadcast licence as permission for every platform. The government’s 2024 expansion announcement sets a 4% annual licence fee for the specified new-city channels. This must not be assumed to replace all existing ENIL licence obligations. S28 The station should establish how renewal exposure and regulatory changes enter deciding where to invest money. Treat potential reforms as scenarios, not approved future savings."}, {"title": "Radio & advertisers · Know the frequency, but check the city", "url": "/radio-mirchi/radio#frequencies", "text": "Know the frequency, but check the city The September MIB list includes Mirchi at 98.3 MHz in Delhi, and at both 98.3 and 95 MHz in Bengaluru. Frequencies and brand variants vary by market; “98.3” is not the complete network map. S35 Before discussing a particular city, verify the current station, format, listener proposition and advertiser set. A second frequency should be evaluated on audience and commercial differentiation rather than assumed to be duplicate reach."}, {"title": "Events & formats · When does an event cover its costs?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events#diagram-1", "text": "When does an event cover its costs? Money comes in Example: ₹3 lakh sponsorship plus ₹2 lakh ticket receipts. Bills come due Example: ₹4.5 lakh for the venue, artists and delivery. Check the result ₹5 lakh income less ₹4.5 lakh costs leaves ₹50,000. Another ₹75,000 in production costs turns it into a ₹25,000 loss. Change the attendance and see when the event breaks even."}, {"title": "Events & formats · A sponsor commits verbally. Would you open ticket sales?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events", "text": "A sponsor commits verbally. Would you open ticket sales? Choose an answer to explore the trade-off. Start selling while interest is high First check cancellation exposure. If deposits are due before sponsor cash arrives, ticket demand alone may leave the organiser short of cash. Test lower attendance Wait for a signed commitment A signature helps, but payment timing matters too. Ask how much cash is due before the venue and artist deposits, and who carries the downside. Compare the event formats"}, {"title": "Events & formats · Know the portfolio and its status", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events#portfolio", "text": "Know the portfolio and its status Property / format Evidence / status Business lesson Mirchi Rock & Dhol 2026 Navratri listings across cities; now historical. S11 Cultural demand can support repeated city editions; pricing and costs remain local. Mirchi Neon Run District lists Chennai, 28 Nov 2026, 6 PM, from ₹299. Upcoming listing at research date. S26 Running plus entertainment can serve consumers and sponsors; verify the listing before attending. Mirchi Freshers S13 Campaign website and terms: 100 colleges, 16 cities, Aug-Sep 2026 period. S12 S13 A campus programme can join physical participation with a digital voting loop. Mirchi Music Awards Established IP; linked 16th-edition results are dated Dec 2024. S15 Recognisable content can generate sponsor and distribution value; no unverified 2026 edition claimed. SBI Green Marathon / Mindspace Eco Run Reported in Q4 FY26 presentation. S4 Commissioned or partner-led programmes can be commercially distinct from owned ticketed concerts. Mirchi Holicious BookMyShow 2026 listing marked event closed. S27 Ticket distribution is a separate relationship from the event brand. Spell Bee, concerts, corporate / RWA activations Established catalogue in company profile. S7 Several smaller formats can serve predictable advertiser needs without marquee concert risk. Royal Stag Boombox / Mirchi Club Nights Reported event / activation cases in Q4 FY26. S4 Different partner formats need different commercial contracts and delivery measures; association does not prove IP ownership. A portfolio mention does not prove ENIL owns every IP, all ticket proceeds or all execution economics. Contract structure must be checked event by event."}, {"title": "Events & formats · The event income and cost sheet", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events#economics", "text": "The event income and cost sheet Revenue / cost line What to verify Sponsorship Signed consideration, barter versus cash, deliverables, collections, cancellation clauses. Ticketing Paid admissions, average actual price earned, discounts, complimentary entries, refunds, platform fees and taxes. Ancillary Only contracted organiser share of stalls, hospitality or merchandise. Direct cost Artist, venue, stage, sound, production, permissions, security, medical, insurance and staffing. Marketing & selling Paid media, commissions, creative and attributable sales cost. Downside Weather, cancellation, artist non-appearance, demand shortfall and vendor commitments. Cash & overhead Timing of advances and settlements; show profit after direct costs separately from allocated corporate overhead. The live calculator uses a simplified pre-tax profit after direct costs model. It is an illustration, not Mirchi’s actual event cost structure. Open event economics lab ."}, {"title": "Events & formats · The attendee journey is the retention product", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events#journey", "text": "The attendee journey is the retention product Discover: relevant city, format, date, artist and accessible information; explain why this event fits the person. Decide: show total price, inclusions, age/access rules, transport information and cancellation terms before checkout. Attend: ticket retrieval, entry throughput, clear help, safe capacity, water and medical support. Remember: useful post-event content and feedback; obtain permission for future contact. Return: recommend the next relevant event, not every event nationwide. Reward repeat attendance only when extra margin pays for the benefit."}, {"title": "Events & formats · Proposed event growth system", "url": "/radio-mirchi/events#scale", "text": "Proposed event growth system Start with one repeatable format in two contrasting cities. Give every edition a signed sponsor plan, a downside budget, a cash exposure ceiling and an attendee service plan. Link the promotion source to a first-party or partner-compliant event record so the team can understand conversion without double-counting. Analysis Before go / no-go Secure minimum sponsorship and venue options. Track break-even paid admissions against realistic demand, then set spend-release gates. Analysis During execution Use one operating checklist with named owners. Escalate queues, safety, artist delays and fulfilment gaps quickly. Capture only the data necessary for the intended service. Analysis After the edition Close collections, vendor settlements and sponsor fulfilment. Compare actual profit after direct costs with the quote and track sponsor / attendee return over subsequent editions."}, {"title": "Users & their needs · Who matters for each buying decision?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/users#priority", "text": "Who matters for each buying decision? Morning show. The commuter chooses the programme. A café buys repeated local ads. A booking code helps count responses, while the station log confirms delivery. This is an illustrative use case. Showroom campaign. The dealer pays, prospective drivers participate and the host introduces the product. Count completed drives, the dealer’s follow-up and the contribution from repeat bookings. College event. Students participate, a sponsor funds agreed activities and organisers manage entry. Check attendance, sponsor delivery and direct costs. Ticket receipts depend on the agreement. Documented client campaigns"}, {"title": "Users & their needs · The customer map", "url": "/radio-mirchi/users#needs", "text": "The customer map Customer Job to be done What good looks like Research to run Local listener / commuter “Keep me company and connected to my city.” Relevant hosts, easy tuning, predictable experience. Diary study by commute / time of day, city and language. Music subscriber “Play something I want, quickly, without interruptions.” Relevant discovery, reliable playback and clear access. Task tests plus funnel and subscriber groups. Story / RJ fan “Let me follow this voice or series wherever I am.” Correct episode, continuity and meaningful release alerts. Follow one series from social discovery to repeat listening. Event attendee “Help me choose a worthwhile, smooth outing.” Clear price and rules, dependable entry and experience. Journey interviews including non-buyers and no-shows. Advertiser / agency “Reach the right people and justify the next budget.” Clear scope, dependable delivery and honest measurement. Win/loss interviews and repeat-spend analysis. Sponsor “Own an audience moment with credible value.” Contracted visibility, engagement and documented fulfilment. Sponsor review before and after each edition. RJ / creator / operations team “Help me make and deliver quality work efficiently.” Fast tools, rights clarity, fair control and fewer handoffs. Observe real production and campaign workflows. These are needs-based hypotheses, not claims from completed customer interviews."}, {"title": "Users & their needs · Segment by behaviour before adding demographics", "url": "/radio-mirchi/users#segmentation", "text": "Segment by behaviour before adding demographics Audio: language, entry intent, content affinity, time of day, device, connection quality and paid status. Events: city, group size, format, first versus repeat attendance, purchase lead time and budget. Advertisers: objective, category, geography, budget, buying frequency, agency involvement and credit profile. Partnership user groups: redemption, listening activation, service duration, renewal eligibility and profit after direct costs. A single retention number can hide a strong existing subscriber base and a weak partner-acquired user group. Define the population and the moment the clock starts before comparing groups."}, {"title": "Users & their needs · Discovery plan with evidence standards", "url": "/radio-mirchi/users#research", "text": "Discovery plan with evidence standards Analysis Qualitative Interview customers who completed the job and those who abandoned it. Observe the task rather than asking which feature they want. Recruit beyond vocal fans and recent complainants. Analysis Quantitative Validate event and account identity, payment outcomes and instrumentation before using funnels. Segment by channel and release version so a rollout does not look like a behavioural trend. Analysis Decision Write the evidence that would change the proposed priority. A hypothesis should be easy to reject when research shows the need is small, already met or too costly to serve."}, {"title": "Web, mobile & apps · What the platform evidence covers", "url": "/radio-mirchi/platforms#test-depth", "text": "What the platform evidence covers Tried in a browser: Mirchi discovery and the handoff to Gaana, regional series pages and a brief web playback start. Mobile web was checked at a simulated phone width. Checked in app stores: listings, descriptions and release notes. Installed-app login, playback after a call, offline listening and purchases still need a device test. Public storefront evidence cannot establish whether these tasks succeed."}, {"title": "Web, mobile & apps · Platform coverage and depth", "url": "/radio-mirchi/platforms#coverage", "text": "Platform coverage and depth Surface What was checked Depth / outcome Mirchi desktop web Root, Reads, navigation, subscription handoff, mobile-app landing. Live browser at default 1280 × 720. Root leads to /stories; Gaana links and app QR visible. S21 S20 Mirchi mobile web Reads and app handoff at 390 × 844. Responsive browser emulation; document width 390px. No physical handset used. S21 Gaana desktop web Home, plan page, radio, podcast listing, series detail and app-store links. Live public journey. Subscription options and sign-in controls inspected; no payment. S22 S23 S24 S25 Gaana mobile web Public discovery and navigation at 390 × 844. Responsive controls observed. Radio document measured 728px wide at 390px viewport; reproduce and isolate overflow before grading severity. S24 Mirchi Android Listing, metadata, update date, rating and description. Live listing inspected. Installed app, OTP, playback and offline mode not tested. S16 Mirchi iOS Website handoff and India / Qatar storefront availability. India URL showed page not found; Qatar listing accessible. No universal delisting conclusion. S17 S20 Gaana Android Listing, current release notes and declared features. Public store evidence. No installed Android / Android Auto test. S18 Gaana iOS / iPad / Watch India listing, version history, supported devices and purchase SKUs. Listing evidence; no native iPhone, iPad, Watch or CarPlay execution. S19 Gaana TV / Phone-maker distribution Samsung TV and Vivo disclosures. Company-reported availability; device-level experience not exercised. S1 Mirchi Live / Freshers Home, campus page, participation form fields and voting information. Live browser. No selfie uploaded, consent accepted, registration or vote submitted. S12 S13 Ticket distribution District Neon Run listing and Book tickets flow; BookMyShow historical event. District ticket types observed before purchase; location prompt encountered. No tickets added or paid. S26 S27 Social / creator distribution Mirchi Bangla YouTube; Mirchi Plus and Gaana Instagram; official Gaana footer links. Public profile / content inspected. Not a census of every city, RJ and language account. S31 S33 S22 Corporate / investor web Company profile, leadership, filings, annual and investor materials. Primary-source document review. S7 S8 S1 S4 S5"}, {"title": "Web, mobile & apps · Observed findings, without overstating impact", "url": "/radio-mirchi/platforms#observations", "text": "Observed findings, without overstating impact Observed / needs validation O1 · Mirchi-to-Gaana transition Mirchi Reads uses Gaana navigation and subscription calls to action; the separate app landing still promotes Mirchi Plus. That is an observed mixed destination story, not proof users are confused. S21 S20 Validate: can a new user explain which app they need and preserve their intended content through the handoff? Measure: destination success and retained listening. Observed / scoped O2 · Mobile accessibility constraint Mirchi Reads declares a viewport with user-scalable=no. At 390px, the first article heading appeared around 522px from the top in this session, following navigation and an ad region. S21 Validate: pinch / browser zoom and first-content visibility on real devices with and without ads. Test: allow zoom and reduce non-content space while monitoring ad profit after direct costs. Observed / region-specific O3 · App availability needs a regional check The India Mirchi iOS URL returned page not found; the app-download landing links a US storefront, while Qatar metadata remains accessible. S17 S20 Validate: the intended India migration / app policy, current installation paths and deep-link fallbacks. A regional unavailable page is not evidence that all apps are discontinued. Observed / checkout untested O4 · Existing plan choice is substantial Gaana web showed trial, recurring and one-time annual choices. Cancellation guidance was present in its public FAQ. S23 Validate: selected renewal amount, mandate comprehension, purchase restore and actual cancellation tasks. Presence of a choice does not prove the complete task works. Observed / partial playback O5 · A regional story plays publicly Sunday Suspense Bangla series and episodes were visible. A brief play test advanced the HTML audio clock with readyState 4. S25 Limit: this demonstrates a browser playback start in this session, not full-length access, audible quality, native playback or offline paid access. Content-level access may vary. Observed / status question O6 · Campaign status needs reconciliation Freshers home displayed a live campus and completed campuses, while published terms gave a tentative September end. S12 S13 Validate: whether terms were extended and ensure the displayed status, eligibility and current legal period agree. Do not label the campaign inactive solely from the tentative date."}, {"title": "Web, mobile & apps · App evidence: current release versus legacy storefront", "url": "/radio-mirchi/platforms#apps", "text": "App evidence: current release versus legacy storefront App / surface Snapshot What it does and does not establish Mirchi Plus Android Live browser: 2.9 stars, ~1.82K reviews, 1M+ downloads; update 30 Mar 2024. S16 A legacy public listing remains. Search cache differed; rating is not a full user-sentiment study. Mirchi Plus iOS Qatar Version 7.5, 1 Apr 2024. S17 Regional metadata only. India availability could not be established. Gaana Android 4.2 stars, 100M+ downloads; retrieved update 4 Nov 2026. S18 Active release metadata and large lifetime distribution. Downloads do not equal active or paying listeners. Gaana iOS India 4.1 stars, ~114K ratings; version 9.26.4 shown two days ago. S19 Current release evidence, supported devices and store SKUs. Not installed-app QA. Reviews: avoid a false finding The public pages surfaced old featured reviews as well as current metadata. I did not scrape a recent, deduplicated representative sample, so I do not claim that historical billing, ads or playback complaints describe the current release."}, {"title": "Web, mobile & apps · Cross-platform test cases to complete with devices and accounts", "url": "/radio-mirchi/platforms#journeys", "text": "Cross-platform test cases to complete with devices and accounts Journey Required checks Business reason Acquire / restore Web, Android, iOS; phone / email identity; existing subscriber restore; partner redemption; source intent. Prevent paid or partner users from losing access. Listen First play, queue, search, language, resume, background, Bluetooth, lock screen, interruption and weak network. Prove the listening habit works in the actual context. Offline / devices Download, airplane mode, expiry, device switch; Watch / TV / car where supported. Verify the paid promise and device-specific continuity. Billing control Full renewal disclosure, mandate, receipt, cancellation, restore and support escalation. Protect trust and reliable recurring revenue. Attend / return Social / radio source, event detail, booking platform, total price, ticket retrieval, entry and post-event communication. Connect conversion to a useful experience and a return relationship. Participate Current eligibility, optional permissions, photo flow, voting rules, error states and consent records. Make campaign participation clear and operationally trustworthy. These cases remain a native / authenticated validation plan. The public audit does not claim they passed."}, {"title": "Current initiatives · When did the main products and campaigns appear?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/initiatives#timeline", "text": "When did the main products and campaigns appear? Period What happened Why it matters FY23 Mirchi Plus was already operating. ENIL discussed its digital audience in its May 2023 results. This establishes that the platform existed by then. FY24 ENIL acquired the Gaana business and began integrating Mirchi Plus with it. An acquisition brought an existing music service into ENIL. FY25 Mirchi Brewery reported MG Hector showroom drives and a Dulux dealer display contest. ENIL FY25 annual report, pp. 15–16 Client campaigns delivered during the year. First launch dates were not disclosed. FY26 ENIL reported work on listening history and lyrics search, alongside events including the Mindspace Eco Run. S4 Product updates and event editions have different measures of success. FY27 through September 2026 ENIL reported Gaana distribution through Vivo and Samsung TV, and the Mirchi Par AI-GIRI campaign. S1 Check whether each distribution deal brings listeners back and whether each campaign delivers for sponsors. This timeline covers documented milestones. Dates are reporting periods unless an exact launch date is available. FY23 results · FY24 annual report ."}, {"title": "Current initiatives · Current and recent initiative register", "url": "/radio-mirchi/initiatives#register", "text": "Current and recent initiative register Filter initiatives All initiatives Gaana product & distribution Mirchi & local campaigns Events & participation Launched, Oct 2026 Noise bundle One-year premium bundle. S10 Question to build on it How many eligible purchasers redeem, activate, retain and later renew? Reported Q2 FY27 Samsung TV + Vivo distribution TV availability; phone pre-installation. S1 Question to build on it Do device user groups produce extra, profitable listening after cost of getting a new customer? Reported Q2 FY27 Queue & mood controls Contextual queue updates. S1 Question to build on it Which contexts improve successful listening and paid retention? Reported Q2 FY27 UMG early access Selected releases 72 hours early. S1 Question to build on it Does exclusivity change paid conversion or just shift listening time? Reported Q2 FY27 DISCVR / Off The Record / Unplugged Artist-led formats. S1 Question to build on it Which formats create retained listeners after production cost? Reported Q4 FY26 Web 2.0 / listening history / lyrics search Web, resumption and discovery upgrades. S4 Question to build on it Have time to successful play and repeat sessions improved? Reported Q2 FY27 Mirchi Par AI-GIRI Delhi AI content campaign. S1 Question to build on it Can engagement become a useful recurring local content franchise? Reported Q2 FY27 Vriksha Bandhan Mumbai tree-adoption campaign. S1 Question to build on it What sponsor and participant outcomes survive after campaign completion? Reported Q2 FY27 Border Podcast Chandigarh listener experience. S1 Question to build on it Which parts are repeatable without losing authenticity or safety? Reported Q2 FY27 Vocal for Local / Telugu Language Day Local business and cultural campaigns. S1 Question to build on it Does the local relationship translate into repeat advertiser or audience value? 2026 campaign; terms Aug-Sep Mirchi Freshers Season 13 Campus participation and voting. S12 Question to build on it What portion of participation converts into sponsor value and permitted repeat engagement? Past October listings Rock & Dhol 2026 Multi-city festive format. S11 Question to build on it Which cities and editions met profit after direct costs and attendee-service targets? Reported Q4 FY26 SBI Green Marathon / Mindspace Eco Run Partner event programmes. S4 Question to build on it How do contractual delivery, repeat sponsorship and cash exposure compare? Upcoming listing: 28 Nov Mirchi Neon Run Chennai Ticketed neon running experience. S26 Question to build on it What is the break-even paid attendance and signed sponsor coverage? Published 17 Nov 2026 Sunday Suspense: Indrapatan YouTube and Gaana release. S14 Question to build on it Can a story-specific handoff increase retained listening?"}, {"title": "Current initiatives · Implications for future investment", "url": "/radio-mirchi/initiatives#respect", "text": "Implications for future investment Confirm the owner, release date and uptake of existing products before commissioning another version. City teams can assess local participation and sponsor delivery. Subscription plans and device distribution require the Gaana team."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Documented use in India", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#india", "text": "Documented use in India For Muthoot Finance’s Maha Kumbh campaign, Mirchi Brewery described using AI to make short films about devotees. The report also describes the locker service and registrations. It gives no separate sales result or production savings from the films. ENIL FY25 annual report, pp. 15–16 For a station pilot, a producer could use AI to suggest clips from a cleared recording, then measure editing time per approved clip. This proposal needs editorial approval. The UAE presenter, device features and Gaana recommendations below have different owners."}, {"title": "AI today & next · One AI idea a producer could test", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#diagram-1", "text": "One AI idea a producer could test Record the interview Start with audio cleared for this use. Suggest short clips AI marks promising moments. The producer checks context and rights. Measure usable clips Compare approved clips per hour, including time spent fixing mistakes. Proposed experiment for a radio team."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Summary of documented uses", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#summary", "text": "Summary of documented uses “Mirchi has already experimented with AI presenters, station branding, campaigns, images and event photo delivery. Gaana also reports new voice controls through Apple. I would build on this work with tools that help teams prepare and reuse content, serve advertisers and help listeners find the right music or story. I would start with one workflow, measure the time and money it saves, and expand after the team finds the output useful.” Existing examples are documented below. Every proposed use is a suggestion for validation. The public record cannot reveal every internal tool, supplier or experiment."}, {"title": "AI today & next · What is already documented", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#existing", "text": "What is already documented Area What the source establishes Status and date Question to ask AI radio presenter Airah in Mirchi UAE, with AI-driven presenting, content and social posts. S39 Historical FY23 disclosure. Is it still running, and what did it change in production cost, audience response and advertiser demand? Station audio and AI education AI-produced station IDs and expert conversations in Delhi’s AI-Giri campaign. Company reported 500K+ Instagram reach. S1 Reported Q2 FY27. How much was a one-off campaign, and what became a regular production tool? Voice control for streaming Mirchi reports Gaana actions through Siri and Apple Intelligence, plus Spotlight discovery. S40 Reported September 2026. Native test pending. Which devices, languages and actions work reliably? Does voice use lead to more successful listening? Event photography AI-powered real-time runner photo delivery at Mindspace Eco Run. S4 Reported Q4 FY26. How are participants matched to photos, what permission is obtained and how accurate and fast is delivery? Visual production An article explicitly labels its illustration as AI generated. S42 Specific August 2026 example. Which teams use image generation, how is work reviewed, and what is the cost per usable image? Editorial assistance An article says AI selected a ranking of Suriya songs. S43 Specific July 2025 example. What editorial review and source checks happen before publication? Campus participation Freshers advertises an AI Remix round using OPPO devices. S12 2026 campaign. Which capability is supplied by the partner, and what result does the sponsor receive? Music recommendations Gaana reports algorithm-led queue improvements, mood controls and personalised discovery. S1 S4 Reported product work. AI model not specified. Which parts use machine learning, and what improvement did testing show? Internal operations Public examples establish selected uses. A complete current inventory of staff tools and suppliers was not verified. Confirm in discovery. Ask content, sales, technology, finance and city teams which AI tools they use and who owns their costs."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Where I would start", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#priority", "text": "Where I would start Order First use Why it is worth testing Decision measure 1 Turn a recorded show into a searchable transcript, suggested clips and draft captions. One recording can support several existing channels. Editors can judge usefulness quickly. Minutes of staff work per approved clip, cost per approved asset, factual errors and return listening. 2 Help sales teams prepare a campaign proposal and delivery report. Uses existing offers, rate cards and campaign records. Reduces time spent assembling information. Time per approved proposal, win rate, delivery errors and profit after serving the client. 3 Help listeners describe the music or story they want in everyday language. Builds on Gaana search and recommendation work. Start with one language and a small catalogue. Successful listening starts, search abandonment, skips and return after 7 and 30 days. 4 Help event teams forecast ticket demand and check costs. Supports decisions before large vendor commitments. Needs clean historical data. Forecast error, event profit, unsold capacity and cash exposed before the event. This order is provisional. I would change it after learning the role’s mandate, current tools, data quality and team capacity. A basic template or rule may solve part of the job more cheaply than AI."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Operations, advertising and event opportunities", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#operations", "text": "Operations, advertising and event opportunities Explore proposed uses and measures Team / task Proposed assistance How it helps What people still approve RJ preparation Search approved local stories, previous shows and guest notes. Draft a short briefing with links. Less preparation time and easier access to the archive. Facts, local relevance, script and on-air delivery. Production Suggest time-coded clips, captions, titles and language-specific versions. More approved output from the same recording. Context, humour, accuracy, talent permission and final edit. Ad delivery Compare broadcast logs and approved scripts with booked spots. Flag missing or mismatched delivery. Fewer missed commitments and faster campaign reporting. Whether an exception needs a replacement spot or client remedy. Sales Draft a proposal from the client’s objective using approved packages and availability. Faster turnaround and more consistent offers. Price, promised results, inventory and contract. Client reporting Assemble delivery evidence, costs and results into a draft report. Less manual reporting and clearer renewal conversations. All performance claims and the invoice. Customer support Find account help and explain the next step using verified plan and payment records. Faster answers for access and billing questions. Refunds, account changes and unusual payment cases. Finance and vendors Match invoice details, flag duplicate bills and highlight overdue collections. Less checking work and earlier visibility of cash gaps. Payments, vendor changes and accounting treatment. Event planning Estimate demand using previous editions, city, format, lead time and sales pace. Show a range. Better decisions on venue size and marketing spend. Budget, venue, capacity and spending commitments. Event service Answer from approved venue, ticket, timing and accessibility information. Faster help before and during an event. Emergency responses and changes to event policy. Audience research Group support themes and listener feedback, then show representative examples. Teams can find recurring needs sooner. The interpretation and whether the sample represents the audience."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Streaming and podcast opportunities", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#streaming", "text": "Streaming and podcast opportunities Explore proposed uses and measures Customer need Proposed experience Business measure Quality check “I know the mood, but not a song.” Describe language, activity and mood, then preview a short playlist built from the licensed catalogue. First successful play, skips, listening days and renewal. Every suggested track must exist and be available to that listener. “I heard a line, but forgot the title.” Improve mixed-language and imperfect-lyric search, building on existing lyrics search. S4 Search success and time to play. Test real misspellings and regional pronunciation with consented samples. “Help me continue my story.” Use the saved listening position and an editor-approved recap before resuming. Episode completion and return to the series. Keep spoilers out and let the listener skip the recap. “Find a story like the one I loved.” Recommend by language, theme, pace and available time using reviewed episode descriptions. Starts that become meaningful listening, then return visits. Test whether recommendations are varied and relevant. “I want to find the part about a topic.” Search approved podcast transcripts and open the exact time in the episode. Successful searches and listening time after the jump. Show the original words and time stamp. Check transcript errors. “I want this in my language.” Create subtitles, translated descriptions or authorised dubbed versions for selected content. New-language listening after translation cost. Language editors and performers approve meaning, pronunciation and voice use. “Help me make a podcast.” Draft chapters, show notes, trailer choices and production checklists from a creator’s recording. Time per published episode and creator return. Creator controls the final voice, edit and publication. “Let me ask about the episode.” Answer questions using only the approved episode transcript and link to the relevant moment. Useful answers that lead to listening or a follow. Say when the episode does not contain the answer. Test accuracy before release."}, {"title": "AI today & next · New products and commercial ideas to validate", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#launches", "text": "New products and commercial ideas to validate Explore proposed uses and measures Idea Who it serves and what they receive Possible way to earn Small first test Mirchi Creator Desk RJs and podcast teams upload a recording and receive editable clips, chapters, captions and show notes. Internal time savings first. A paid creator service could follow if external demand is established. Use one show and one editor. Compare total production time and approved output with the current process. Gaana Listen My Way Listeners describe a situation in everyday language and receive a playable selection. Improved subscription use and renewal. Test paid demand only after proving the listening benefit. One language, one device and the existing catalogue. Compare with current search and playlists. Mirchi Local Brand Studio A local business gives its city, offer and budget. The sales team receives an editable radio and social campaign draft. Campaign fee, repeat advertiser spend and optional production charges. One advertiser category in two cities, with approved offers and human sales review. Mirchi Story Explorer Fans search a licensed story catalogue by topic, author or available time, then open a relevant episode. More repeat listening, subscriptions or a sponsor-funded discovery experience. One rights-cleared series. Test search completion and return over several weeks. Mirchi Event Planner City teams see ticket demand ranges, sponsor commitments, costs and unresolved tasks in one view. Better event profit and lower unnecessary spending. Use historical editions first, then compare forecasts with actual ticket sales. Artist Fan Assistant Artists receive help tagging uploads, drafting multilingual posts and understanding fan questions. Optional creator tools or transactions under agreed partner terms. Check the announced FENIX360 roadmap first, then test one support task with artists. S41 AI-Giri at Work Companies or campuses book practical AI sessions hosted by Mirchi talent with subject experts. Workshop fees and relevant sponsorship. Build on AI-Giri. Sell a small pilot and measure attendance, learning and repeat bookings. S1 These are proposed product concepts. Names describe the ideas and do not indicate announced Mirchi launches. Price, demand, technical cost and rights need validation before a business case is approved."}, {"title": "AI today & next · How to judge the money and time saved", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#value", "text": "How to judge the money and time saved For staff tools, count the complete task. Include checking and correcting AI output. Saving drafting time helps only when total time per approved result falls. For streaming, compare similar groups receiving the new and existing experience. Track successful plays, return after 7 and 30 days, subscription renewal and profit after content, payment, support and AI costs. For advertising, track approved proposal time, win rate, price achieved, delivery, collections and repeat spend. Include whether a new package takes money away from an existing package. For events, measure demand forecast error, ticket income, refunds, sponsor renewal and actual profit after direct costs. Count vendor commitments made before cash is collected. For a new paid AI product, test whether customers pay and return. Include model usage, audio processing, storage, editing, support and sales costs. Illustrative calculation. If 20 editors each save 30 minutes a day over 22 working days, that is 220 hours per month. At an assumed ₹500 per hour, the capacity value is ₹1.10 lakh. After an assumed ₹30,000 monthly tool and review cost, the model leaves ₹80,000 in capacity value. These are example inputs. Actual cash savings depend on how the freed time is used."}, {"title": "AI today & next · The first 90 days for AI", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#delivery", "text": "The first 90 days for AI Period Work Evidence needed to continue Days 1-15 Map tools already used by each team. Observe real tasks. Record time, errors, cost and who approves the result. One repeated task with a clear owner and enough examples to evaluate. Days 16-30 Build the content assistant or proposal assistant around approved source material. Compare it with a template and the current workflow. Useful output after review, with acceptable cost and fewer total staff minutes. Days 31-60 Run a limited team pilot. Track corrections, rejected outputs and time per completed task. The team keeps using it and the whole task becomes cheaper or faster without a quality decline. Days 61-90 Expand the successful workflow. Test a small listener-facing idea if the data, rights and team are ready. Measured customer benefit, an owner for ongoing support and a clear spending limit. A transcript-and-clip assistant is my provisional first choice because the team can review its output and measure its value quickly. A large new voice or music product needs stronger proof of demand and rights before investment."}, {"title": "AI today & next · Questions I would ask the Mirchi team", "url": "/radio-mirchi/ai#questions", "text": "Questions I would ask the Mirchi team Which AI tools already save staff time, and how is that measured? Is Airah still in use, and what did the experiment teach the team? Which Gaana discovery and voice features use AI, and what improved in controlled tests? How much does it cost to produce and repurpose one approved episode or campaign today? What permissions cover talent voices, stories, music, transcripts and new language versions? Who owns the AI budget, output quality and ongoing support? Which customer or business result should the first AI pilot improve?"}, {"title": "What I would improve · Measure local advertiser renewals", "url": "/radio-mirchi/opportunities#advertiser", "text": "Measure local advertiser renewals Offer a limited catalogue by objective, city and budget, supported by a clear delivery plan and response measurement. Start with a category that repeats frequently enough to evaluate within a practical window. A sales-assisted workflow is a reasonable first version; self-service comes after the offer is repeatable. Primary: extra 90-day advertiser profit after direct costs and repeat booking. Checks that protect service and profit: collection days, deal discount, delivery exceptions, sales time and listener experience. Evidence: matched accounts or a phased rollout; track displaced radio or solution spend. Stop: higher gross billings with lower retained margin or longer credit exposure."}, {"title": "What I would improve · Evaluate repeat events", "url": "/radio-mirchi/opportunities#events", "text": "Evaluate repeat events Put event discovery, booking partners, sponsor fulfilment and the event budget into one operating view. Preserve attendee consent and ticket-platform contractual boundaries. The product should support a faster, better proceed or stop decision, not merely make another listing page. Primary: actual edition profit after direct costs and cash conversion. Customer: entry wait, issue resolution, sponsor fulfilment and subsequent return. Scale: comparable editions meet economics and service gates across cities. Stop: the model depends on uncontracted sponsorship, unrealistic attendance or unsustainable local workload."}, {"title": "What I would improve · Improve content discovery and production", "url": "/radio-mirchi/opportunities#ai", "text": "Improve content discovery and production For content continuity, start with one franchise and preserve the destination from social post to story page to app. For AI, observe a real workflow first: transcription, clip selection, tagging, local adaptation or campaign report assembly may be candidates. Compare turnaround and error rate against the current process. Quality and rights are part of the product Keep editorial review, talent permission, licensed-use boundaries and factual checks in the release criteria. A faster output is valuable only if it is usable and creates no extra correction or rights burden."}, {"title": "First 90 days · The first 90 days at a station", "url": "/radio-mirchi/plan#diagram-1", "text": "The first 90 days at a station Days 1–30 Listen to shows and meet listeners, producers and local advertisers. Days 31–60 Test one change in one city. Agree on the outcome before starting. Days 61–90 Check the result and cost. Decide whether another city should try it. Illustrative plan. Choose the pilot with the station team."}, {"title": "First 90 days · The plan", "url": "/radio-mirchi/plan#sequence", "text": "The plan Window Work Concrete output Gate Days 1-15 Agree mandate; meet commercial, content, events, digital, finance and city teams. Observe customer and internal journeys. Business tree, metric definitions, platform inventory, starting result data-quality review. One accountable owner and reliable success measure per pilot. Days 16-30 Size the top two needs; validate existing work; agree estimate of what would have happened without the change and cash exposure. Pilot briefs, starting result user groups, tracking design, cost / benefit ranges, stop rules. Approve one consumer and one commercial pilot only if capacity permits. Days 31-60 Ship bounded changes; add tracking for paid access and delivery; monitor service quality. Working activation journey or advertiser workflow; weekly economics review. Results are measurable and checks that protect service and profit stable before more traffic or cities. Days 61-90 Read mature-enough user groups; close sponsor/vendor cash loops; compare rollout groups. Decision memo: scale, iterate or stop; owner, budget and next-quarter plan. Scale by extra profit after direct costs and customer value, not activity counts. Annual Gaana renewal cannot be proved in 90 days. Use short-term activation and retention as leading indicators, maintain the user group for later renewal measurement and avoid claiming validated lifetime value too early."}, {"title": "First 90 days · Operating cadence", "url": "/radio-mirchi/plan#cadence", "text": "Operating cadence Analysis Weekly business review A compact dashboard: commercial profit after direct costs and collections; consumer activation and retention; delivery exceptions; experiment status; key dependencies. Discuss decisions and surprises, not a catalogue of activities. Analysis Fortnightly learning review Show one customer journey or workflow observation, test results and what changed in the hypothesis. Include negative evidence and experiments stopped. Analysis Monthly portfolio review Compare investment and returns by business. Protect committed delivery, revisit capital exposure and stop pilots that are not earning the next tranche of effort."}, {"title": "First 90 days · Collaboration and who can make each decision", "url": "/radio-mirchi/plan#stakeholders", "text": "Collaboration and who can make each decision Partner What to agree Finance Revenue definitions, profit after direct costs waterfall, cash timing and investment hurdle. Sales & agencies Customer need, offer scope, pricing authority and renewal ownership. Content / RJs Editorial quality, local adaptation, workload and talent rights. Events & city teams Safety and service requirements, vendors, venue constraints and escalation owners. Engineering / design / data Smallest release, instrumentation quality and operational support. Partnerships / legal Paid access terms, distribution measurement and permitted data use. Dependencies are part of prioritisation. A high-impact idea that requires unready billing, rights or partner data may need a smaller first step."}, {"title": "First 90 days · What I would resolve before promising a roadmap", "url": "/radio-mirchi/plan#questions", "text": "What I would resolve before promising a roadmap Which business and budget does this role own? What outcome would make the hiring manager call the first quarter successful? Which teams and systems can the role directly influence? What product work is already committed or in flight? Which metrics are trusted, and where do teams disagree on definitions? Which city / audience / advertiser segment is most strategically important now?"}, {"title": "How to measure success · The main measures and what drives them", "url": "/radio-mirchi/metrics#tree", "text": "The main measures and what drives them Business Outcome Leading measures Checks that protect service and profit Radio Profit after direct costs from retained advertisers Actual price earned, percentage of ad time sold, repeat spend, delivery. Ad load, discount leakage, credit days, make-goods. Events Actual edition profit after direct costs and cash recovery Paid attendance, actual income per ticket after deductions, sponsor collections, repeat intent. Safety, entry wait, refunds, vendor exposure, fulfilment. Gaana Profit from subscribers who stay Intent-to-play, paid access success, retained listening days, renewal user groups. Playback reliability, support cost, refunds, partner economics. Digital solutions Profit kept after delivery costs per client Proposal speed, win rate, repeat scope, on-time delivery. Pass-through spend, rework, collections and concentration. Content Useful retained audience and attributable commercial value Repeat consumption, series completion, qualified handoffs. Rights, quality, creator workload and platform dependence. A proposed shared lens is extra profit after direct costs from repeat customer relationships . It needs a business-specific definition; a sponsor, an advertiser and a subscriber cannot share one base number."}, {"title": "How to measure success · Definitions that need to be explicit", "url": "/radio-mirchi/metrics#definitions", "text": "Definitions that need to be explicit Activation: a successful value moment, not an install or a login. Define the required listening or campaign outcome before measurement. Retention: the fraction of a starting user group that meets the defined value event in a later window. Keep trial, partner and paid user groups separate. Profit after direct costs: net recorded revenue minus the costs included in the agreed cost breakdown. State which variable, direct and allocated costs are included. Extra results caused by the change: the change versus a credible estimate of what would have happened without the change, not the number of people with a campaign tag. Repeat event customer: one person attending a second relevant edition, counted once using information they have permitted us to link. Actual price earned: actual revenue after discounts and the adjustments agreed with finance."}, {"title": "How to measure success · The important rates, with simple formulas", "url": "/radio-mirchi/metrics#rate-formulas", "text": "The important rates, with simple formulas Measure How to calculate it What to keep consistent First-play conversion People with a successful first play ÷ eligible people who entered the journey × 100. Use people, not a mixture of visits, installs and accounts. Define a successful play. 7-day / 30-day return People from the starting group who listen in the chosen later window ÷ people in that starting group × 100. State whether you mean exactly day 7, any day in week 2, or a rolling window. Episode completion Eligible episode starts that reach the agreed completion point ÷ eligible starts × 100. State the completion threshold; report episode length and repeated starts separately. Paid renewal Subscriptions that renew successfully ÷ subscriptions due to renew in the period × 100. Separate annual, monthly, trial and partner plans. A cancelled future renewal is not always immediate loss of access. Revenue per paying user Subscription revenue for a period ÷ the agreed average number of paying users in that period. State monthly or annual, exclude or include taxes consistently, and separate paying from active users. Customer acquisition cost Included marketing, partner and incentive spend ÷ new paying customers from that spend. A device shipped or a free trial started is not a paying customer. Radio ad-time sold Ad seconds sold ÷ ad seconds available for sale × 100. Compare the same city, station, time of day and period. Radio price earned Recorded ad revenue after agreed adjustments ÷ ad seconds sold. Use the same treatment of agency fees, discounts and bundles. Advertiser repeat rate Advertisers who buy again in the chosen window ÷ advertisers eligible to buy again × 100. Also measure repeat spend and profit: one small repeat order can hide lost budget. Booking conversion Completed paid bookings ÷ eligible booking journeys started × 100. Track people, orders and tickets separately; a couple ticket is not one attendee. Paid attendance Paid-ticket holders who enter ÷ valid paid-ticket holders expected × 100. Separate complimentary passes, refunds, no-shows and capacity. Sponsor renewal Eligible sponsors who book the next comparable edition ÷ eligible sponsors × 100. Also compare retained sponsorship value, cash collected and delivery costs. Event direct profit Sponsor income + ticket income after deductions + other contracted income − included event delivery costs. List the included costs. Direct profit is not the same as final company profit. AI usable-output rate AI outputs accepted after the agreed review ÷ outputs reviewed × 100. Include review time, correction time, rights checks and serious errors when judging value."}, {"title": "How to measure success · Three experiments worth designing properly", "url": "/radio-mirchi/metrics#experiments", "text": "Three experiments worth designing properly Experiment Primary test Design caution Partner activation journey Extra profit from users who keep returning per eligible user group. Randomise or phase within comparable partner user groups; preserve offer and season. Advertiser package 90-day repeat profit after direct costs versus current offer. Account for sales selection, account size and spending shifted from an existing offer. Event reminder / return loop Extra net ticket profit after direct costs from permitted audience. Use a comparison group that does not receive the change; include discount cost, refunds and substitution between events. Choose sample size from the actual starting result, the smallest improvement worth detecting, allocation and desired statistical precision. No sample-size number is invented here. Predefine the decision window and checks that protect service and profit, and avoid repeatedly stopping the test at the first positive result."}, {"title": "How to measure success · How to measure results across offline and online", "url": "/radio-mirchi/metrics#attribution", "text": "How to measure results across offline and online On-air codes, tagged links, unique landing pages, booking references and matching customer records can identify observed response. They do not automatically capture all radio exposure or establish causation. Pair response tracking with comparison groups that do not receive the change or matched comparisons where feasible, and report uncertainty. The stakeholder-friendly dashboard Show what was delivered, what people did, what changed versus the comparison and how much profit after direct costs remained. Keep these four layers separate so a reach metric does not quietly become a revenue claim."}, {"title": "Try the numbers · Event edition: profit after direct costs and break-even", "url": "/radio-mirchi/lab#events", "text": "Event edition: profit after direct costs and break-even All amounts are in rupees, excluding GST. Ticket price is net organiser receipt; no fees or taxes are deducted again. Variable cost is per paid attendee. Sponsorship and ancillary income must be contracted; allocated corporate overhead is excluded. Paid ticket capacity Paid occupancy (%) Net receipt per ticket (₹) Sponsorship (₹) Net ancillary income (₹) Fixed direct costs (₹) Variable cost per attendee (₹) Reset event assumptions Formula: profit after direct costs = paid attendees × (net ticket receipt − variable cost) + sponsorship + ancillary − fixed costs. Break-even attendees = ceiling(max(0, fixed − sponsorship − ancillary) ÷ ticket profit after direct costs), subject to positive ticket profit after direct costs and capacity."}, {"title": "Try the numbers · Subscriber group: first-year profit after direct costs", "url": "/radio-mirchi/lab#subscribers", "text": "Subscriber group: first-year profit after direct costs This model starts with paying users. It is not suitable for treating a complimentary hardware bundle as full-price revenue. Annual price below is net recorded service revenue per paid year, excluding taxes. Cost-share and retention inputs are hypothetical, not Gaana disclosures. Starting paid users Annual net service revenue (₹) One-year paid renewal (%) Variable revenue-linked costs (%) Annual service cost per user (₹) Cost of getting a new customer per starting user (₹) Reset subscriber assumptions Formula: annual unit profit after direct costs before acquisition = annual net revenue × (1 − variable cost share) − annual service cost. First-year user group profit after direct costs subtracts acquisition spend. Renewal illustration applies the same revenue and costs for each customer to renewing users, excludes new acquisition and is not lifetime value."}, {"title": "Try the numbers · Radio: percentage of ad time sold versus price", "url": "/radio-mirchi/lab#inventory", "text": "Radio: percentage of ad time sold versus price An illustrative period with a fixed pool of available ad seconds. Changing utilisation and price earned does not account for changes in station, time of day, inventory quality, collections or extra operating costs. Available ad seconds Percentage of ad time sold (%) Net revenue per second (₹) Alternative percentage of ad time sold (%) Alternative price discount (%) Reset radio assumptions Decision rule A revenue increase does not establish a profit increase. Use these models to expose assumptions, then add the actual cost, cash and customer constraints before recommending a decision."}, {"title": "Business case · The decision facing Mirchi Digital", "url": "/radio-mirchi#brief", "text": " When a listener hears a Mirchi host on FM or sees a clip online, can Mirchi help them find the full show, return next week and eventually pay for more? Start with one city and one recurring show. Mirchi supplies the host, programme and promotion. A Gaana product partner would own changes to its player, plans and billing.  In July–September 2026 ENIL reported ₹33 crore in digital revenue, up 4.6% year on year and equal to 29% of domestic revenue. Digital still recorded an EBITDA loss of ₹7.5 crore, compared with ₹9.8 crore a year earlier. EBITDA measures earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation. Management flagged a possible one- to two-quarter delay to Gaana’s breakeven aim because bringing in customers cost more.  ENIL earnings call, November 2026  "}, {"title": "Business case · Who needs a better product?", "url": "/radio-mirchi#problem", "text": "   The listener  A city commuter knows the host’s voice but may not know the show title. A short link should open the episode the host just mentioned.    The subscriber  A listener arriving through earbuds or a trial needs a reason to return after the offer ends. A saved local show can give them one.    The advertiser  A local buyer wants to know whether people listened, enquired or visited. Each outcome needs its own count and cost.    ENIL reported 153.1 million monthly active users across its digital properties and around 20 million for Gaana. These have different scopes and potentially overlapping audiences. Dividing one by the other would give a misleading conversion rate.  ENIL earnings call, November 2026  "}, {"title": "Business case · Why start with returning listeners?", "url": "/radio-mirchi#readout", "text": " Gaana moved to paid streaming in 2022 while rivals could still introduce people to music for free. In YouGov’s urban India survey, Gaana usage fell from 32% to 22% between 2021 and 2024, while Spotify rose from 24% to 42%. A familiar city host is one reason to choose Mirchi that a competing music catalogue cannot reproduce.  See the four-service comparison    Which alternatives were considered?  A cheaper weekly pass may attract buyers who would have bought the monthly plan. Buying installs can increase acquisition cost. A local series can use existing distribution, but still costs money to produce and promote. Test repeat use first, then test a paid offer with listeners who have experienced the show.  "}, {"title": "Business case · The proposed listening journey", "url": "/radio-mirchi#solution", "text": "   Discover  Hear a short address on FM or tap a tagged link on a social clip.    Listen  Open the exact episode in the app or a lightweight web player. Offer city and language selection only when useful.    Return  Save the show and resume it. Ask permission for a reminder when the next episode arrives.    Choose a plan  Explain the price, billing period and renewal before purchase. Offer a relevant series or a longer ad-free session.    The parallel advertiser opportunity is to sell sponsorship for a defined audience with evidence of delivery. Obtain rights and confirm eligible placements before quoting.  What M-Ping, Brewery and event sponsorship sell  "}, {"title": "Business case · How to run a controlled pilot", "url": "/radio-mirchi#pilot", "text": " Weeks 1–2: the station producer selects a rights-cleared show. The digital team tests links on mobile web and installed apps. Analytics defines an eligible visitor and records playback, return and payment. Weeks 3–6: compare a direct episode landing page with the usual destination, using random assignment within each tagged source. Keep the same creative, programme and offer. Report FM and social separately. Weeks 7–10: follow each group for 30 days. Compare like-for-like listeners before extending the trial.   How much can the team spend?  Illustration: ₹30,000 in production and promotion divided by 300 additional paying listeners is ₹100 per new payer. At ₹25 a month left after variable costs per active payer, that takes four paid months to recover before churn. If many listeners cancel in month two, the campaign can lose money even with strong sign-ups.  Explore the payback scenarios   "}, {"title": "Business case · When should Mirchi expand?", "url": "/radio-mirchi#decision", "text": " Primary decision: expand only if the test produces more returning listeners and positive additional contribution over the agreed payback window. Track first plays per eligible arrival, seven-day return per first-time listener, first paid renewal per eligible subscriber and contribution after rights, production, partner fees and promotion. Set the minimum useful lift and sample size with finance and analytics before launch.  Pause if playback failures, refund requests or complaints rise beyond the pre-agreed limits. A positive conversion result with weaker renewal calls for a longer observation window. If the listener trial succeeds, take a separately costed sponsorship package to a city advertiser.  Explore an advertiser campaign review  "}, {"title": "Business case · Research appendix", "url": "/radio-mirchi#map", "text": "   Understand  Business & leaders  Follow one morning show from the listener’s seat to the advertiser’s desk, then see how Mirchi worked with four real businesses.    Understand  How Mirchi earns  Follow each source of income: radio ads, brand campaigns, events, digital services and Gaana subscriptions.    Understand  Financial results  The group reports these figures. Use the specifically labelled radio figures when discussing the radio business.    Businesses  Radio & advertisers  Understand paid ad time, pricing, local audiences and how to help advertisers get more value.    Businesses  Events & formats  The main event formats, how their income and costs work, and how to make people and sponsors return.    Businesses  Gaana & digital  Gaana is an ENIL business. For Radio Mirchi, the relevant question is how listeners find and return to its shows when content travels across services.    Understand  Users & their needs  A simple map of listeners, subscribers, event attendees, advertisers and sponsors, plus the questions I would test.    Understand  Market & competitors  Compare the "}, {"title": "Financial results · How much of ENIL’s domestic revenue came from digital?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#digital-mix", "text": " Compare the same July–September quarter each year. The figures below are approximate shares from the supplied digital revenue study. The latest quarter includes Gaana and digital solutions. The 2023 acquisition changes the comparison across years.     Quarter  Digital share of domestic revenue      Q2 FY24     3.2%    Q2 FY25     11.5%    Q2 FY26     23.3%    Q2 FY27     29%     In Q2 FY27 digital earned ₹33 crore. The supplied study estimates roughly ₹22 crore from Gaana and ₹11 crore from digital solutions. Treat this split as an estimate. A rising share can reflect both digital growth and weakness elsewhere. Digital revenue grew 4.6% year on year, while the digital operating loss narrowed to ₹7.5 crore.  Quarterly earnings discussion  "}, {"title": "Financial results · What happened to Indian radio advertising?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#radio-results", "text": " In July–September 2026, selling radio ad time generated less revenue. Management reported a 12.6% decline in commercial airtime revenue. Radio contributed 53% of domestic revenue. Applying this rounded share to ₹113.65 crore gives approximately ₹60 crore for radio. This is an estimate. The 16.1% decline in total domestic sales covers several businesses.  Earnings call  "}, {"title": "Financial results · What these numbers mean", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#plain-financials", "text": "     Measure  Read it as      Revenue  Sales before costs.    EBITDA  Profit before interest, tax and the accounting charges that spread asset costs over years. It is not final profit or cash flow.    PAT  Profit after tax: the final profit or loss.    Domestic / consolidated  The domestic scope shown by ENIL / the group including subsidiaries. Do not mix their numbers.    Q2 FY27 / YoY  July to September 2026 / compared with the same quarter last year.    ₹ crore  ₹1 crore = ₹10 million.      Example: a company can sell more but make less profit if its costs grow faster. It can also improve EBITDA and still report a final loss. "}, {"title": "Financial results · Latest quarter: Q2 FY27", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#quarter", "text": " Period: 1 July-30 September 2026. Released 3 November 2026. ₹ crore; ₹10 million = ₹1 crore. Figures below are presentation-defined operating income/revenue and EBITDA.   S1        Metric  Q2 FY27  Q2 FY26  YoY      Consolidated operating revenue  116.70  141.14  −17.3%    Consolidated EBITDA  12.05  11.38  +5.9%    Consolidated PAT  −4.86  −4.08  Loss widened    Domestic operating revenue  113.65  135.42  −16.1%    Domestic EBITDA  11.26  10.28  +9.5%       Do not mix the headlines  ₹11.3 crore / +9.5% is the rounded domestic EBITDA headline. ₹12.05 crore / +5.9% is consolidated EBITDA in the deck. Pair each profit number with the matching revenue scope.   S1   S2    "}, {"title": "Financial results · Full-year and half-year context", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#year", "text": "     Scope / period  Operating revenue  EBITDA  PAT      Consolidated FY26  565.17  44.96  −7.39    Consolidated FY25  544.15  78.56  11.95    Consolidated H1 FY27  230.39  21.08  −10.86    Consolidated H1 FY26  258.08  18.95  −9.36      FY26 from the Q4 deck; H1 from the Q2 deck. FY26 EBITDA declined despite revenue growth; H1 FY27 EBITDA improved while revenue declined. Annual results and a six-month period are not directly comparable.   S4   S1    Calculated consolidated EBITDA margin: Q2 FY27  10.33%  versus  8.06%  a year earlier; FY26  7.96%  versus FY25  14.44% . These are ratios of the displayed operating figures. EBITDA is not cash flow or PAT. "}, {"title": "Financial results · What the portfolio mix says", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#mix", "text": " Management reported approximately 53% radio and 29% digital in domestic Q2 revenue; the remaining approximately 18% covers events and other business. Digital revenue was about ₹33 crore and digital EBITDA loss about ₹7.5 crore. This is a rounded management mix, not a statutory segment reconciliation.   S2     Radio 53%  Digital 29%  Other 18%   Digital’s share can increase when radio declines. A higher percentage alone does not establish strong absolute digital growth. Evaluate growth, loss reduction and the quality of the acquired user groups together. "}, {"title": "Financial results · Cash and operating costs", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#cash", "text": " September consolidated cash was reported at ₹371.5 crore. For March, the Q4 deck distinguishes consolidated cash of about ₹423.9 crore from standalone cash of ₹404.2 crore. Avoid presenting them as two estimates of the same scope.   S2   S4      Operating leverage:  station and platform costs do not fall instantly with ad demand. Efficiency can improve EBITDA before revenue recovers.   Below EBITDA:  depreciation/amortisation, finance costs, other income, exceptional items and tax can move PAT differently.   Event working capital:  vendor advances create cash exposure before ticket and sponsor collections.   Do not infer runway:  cash balances are not an unrestricted product budget; commitments, lease liabilities and investment plans matter.    Business interpretation  “I see improving operating discipline, but I would not declare the transformation complete. I would track profit after direct costs and cash by business and user group, while protecting the radio engine and proving repeatable economics in digital and events.”  "}, {"title": "Financial results · How sales and profit changed year by year", "url": "/radio-mirchi/financials#annual-trend", "text": "     Financial year  Group operating income (₹ crore)  Growth over last year  Operating earnings before interest, tax and depreciation (₹ crore)  Profit after tax (₹ crore)      FY24, restated  538.43  See note below  105.87  32.98    FY25  544.15  +1.1%  78.56  11.95    FY26  565.17  +3.9%  44.96  −7.39      Sales rose for two years while operating earnings fell. By FY26, ENIL recorded a loss after tax. FY24 originally reported about ₹500 crore of group revenue, up 13.6% on its then-reported comparison. ENIL later revised the FY24 figures to include the acquired business. Use ₹538.43 crore when comparing FY25 with FY24. The FY27 half-year and quarter appear separately above.   FY25 presentation, restated consolidated comparison  ·  Original FY24 release    S4   S1   "}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · What does each platform do for a listener?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#mirchi-plus", "text": "  Mirchi makes the show. Mirchi Plus gives it an on-demand home. Gaana can distribute audio and sell subscriptions.  ENIL operates the businesses. A Mirchi team can commission a series and promote it on air. Placement, entitlement and billing on Gaana require agreement with its product team.   Mirchi Plus on phones, the web and in cars  Launched in India in July 2022 with mobile web, Android and iOS. Launch coverage described short videos, original audio stories and podcasts in English, Hindi, Punjabi, Marathi, Gujarati, Bangla, Telugu, Tamil, Malayalam and Kannada. Its help centre lists Android Auto and Apple CarPlay. In the car, the useful task is starting or resuming audio hands-free. Video discovery belongs on a phone. Current device, regional catalogue and sign-in behaviour need an installed-device check.  Mirchi Plus help centre  ·  ENIL launch presentation  ·  Launch language list     Three journeys to test  A commuter hears a host mention a series and wants the exact episode. A social viewer taps a clip on mobile web and wants to listen before installing. A bundled Gaana list"}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · How Gaana went from early leader to challenger", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#gaana-history", "text": "   2011 · Listening on the web  Gaana let people choose songs online. Its first month attracted about 1.2 million unique visitors and 11.7 million plays. One person could play many songs.    2018–20 · Competing for scale  A $115 million funding round backed expansion. Spotify launched in India in 2019. By 2020 Gaana reported 185 million monthly active users.    2022 · A different price of entry  Gaana ended free streaming and asked listeners to subscribe. A casual listener could keep using rival free services without making that decision. Mirchi separately launched Mirchi Plus for original shows.    2023–26 · Rebuilding paid listening  ENIL acquired the Gaana undertaking in December 2023. By the September 2026 quarter, management was discussing the cost of attracting paying users and the timing of breakeven.    Read the contemporary evidence.  Early web usage  ·  Funding and reported reach  ·  2022 paid pivot  ·  ENIL FY25 annual report  ·  ENIL earnings call, November 2026  "}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · Why did Spotify gain while Gaana fell?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#listener-choice", "text": "  Share of urban Indian respondents using each service    Service  2021  2024  Change      Gaana     32%     22%  -10 points    Spotify     24%     42%  +18 points    JioSaavn     30%     24%  -6 points    YouTube     66%     53%  -13 points     Spotify moved ahead of Gaana in this survey. Respondents could use several services. The survey cannot tell us whether a particular listener switched apps or why. YouTube here is the survey’s YouTube category.  YouGov India Media Morph, 2024    What made Spotify easier to choose?  Free listening reduced the risk of trying it. Spotify also invested in local discovery. It reported that international music accounted for nearly 70% of listening at launch in India, while local music exceeded 70% five years later. A listener who found familiar music, built playlists and kept returning had a reason to stay. The paywall change and local discovery are plausible explanations. Neither explains a measured fraction of Gaana’s decline.  Spotify India at five years     What does the 300 million headline tell us?  Spotify reached 300 million paying subscribe"}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · What can an advertiser buy from Mirchi?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#brand-partnerships", "text": " A brand can pay for access to an audience, for a creative team to make something, or for an event that people attend. A contract may combine all three. Count its revenue once.   M-Ping · buy targeted advertising  A local retailer can specify the audience and pay to deliver a recorded message across participating publishers. Software selects eligible placements. Ads may run before or during audio. A companion banner gives a listener something to tap where supported. Confirm targeting, publisher access and dynamic insertion in the current order. Measure completed listens, cost per thousand impressions and the cost of a qualified response.  M-Ping  ·  Mirchi launch announcement     Mirchi Brewery · commission a campaign  The creative team develops the idea and produces the work. Gradeup used Brewery for a series of brand films. A buyer might commission a host-led reel, a branded audio drama or a podcast segment. Price production, talent and distribution separately. Check completion, qualified enquiries and the money left after production costs.  ENIL FY25 annual report  ·  Gradeup camp"}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · Three digital jobs, three business models", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#portfolio", "text": "   Subscription  Gaana  A consumer pays for a useful audio experience. Discovery, playback reliability, paid access and repeat listening determine value and renewal. The annual report describes a subscription-led service.   S5      Audience & content  Mirchi content  Articles, personalities, stories and social clips attract attention and can lead into deeper listening. The Mirchi web root currently leads to Reads with Gaana navigation.   S21      B2B services  Digital solutions  A brand pays for a campaign or service. The product is reliable creative and commercial delivery, not necessarily another consumer application.   S2     "}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · What the public price surfaces showed", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#pricing", "text": "     Surface  Observed price / proposition  Interpretation      Gaana web trial  ₹1 / 30-day trial; says yearly auto-renew after trial.  Verify the next charge and mandate terms at checkout; no checkout completed.    Gaana web yearly  ₹709 offer; ₹799 reference price.  An observed offer, not actual Average revenue per user.    Gaana web monthly  ₹129; monthly auto-renew.  A different duration and commitment.    Gaana web one-time annual  ₹899; no auto debit.  Existing customer choice; do not propose this as absent.    Noise × Gaana  ₹1,399 hardware bundle with one year Gaana Plus.  Separate hardware price from consideration earned by Gaana.    Gaana iOS listing  SKUs include ₹119 monthly / ₹799 yearly.  Listing SKUs can differ from web and selected checkout eligibility.      Web prices from this session, bundle from the Oct announcement, iOS SKUs from the India listing. Taxes, eligibility and offer duration must be checked in the selected purchase journey.   S23   S10   S19   "}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · Proposed activation and retention sequence", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#growth", "text": "     Stage  Customer value  Measurement      Intent  Preserve the artist, song, podcast or language that brought the user.  Source-linked intent to a successful content start.    Activation  Get to a satisfying listening session quickly.  Time to first successful play; paid access success.    Habit  Make returning easy through existing history, queue and discovery capabilities.  Retained listening days and successful sessions per user group.    Paid value  Explain what the selected plan gives and when it renews.  Profit after direct costs per paid user, disputes and refund requests.    Renewal  Reconnect the person with value they used; easy account control.  Profit from subscription renewals after costs and incentives.      History and queue improvements already appear in company updates. The opportunity is to validate which users benefit and whether those benefits change retention.   S4   "}, {"title": "Gaana & digital · Content IP can bridge attention and payment", "url": "/radio-mirchi/digital#content", "text": " Sunday Suspense continues to publish: the 17 November Indrapatan announcement points audiences to Mirchi Bangla YouTube and Gaana. This is evidence of an active regional franchise, not an estimate of its commercial performance.   S14    Proposed test: preserve a listener’s chosen story or creator through a web-to-app handoff, resume them at the relevant destination and measure qualified listening and subsequent renewal. Keep a clean comparison group that does not receive the change to distinguish genuine extra activity from users who would have listened anyway.   Do not equate these metrics  Social followers, YouTube subscribers, monthly active listeners, trials, paid subscribers and billed subscribers measure different populations. Never use one as a substitute for another, and do not add platform counts into unique reach.  "}, {"title": "Market & competitors · Benchmark: one Delhi dealer seeking weekend test drives", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#dealer", "text": " For the change in listener behaviour, start with the India survey, the dated price comparison and the distinction between reported usage and inferred switching.  See the four-service comparison   Compare what a buyer can establish before asking for a quote. Each operator receives the same brief: a city show, a host appearance, digital follow-up and a record of delivery.    Mirchi.  Its MG Hector work demonstrates host-led showroom execution. Ask for Delhi schedules, replacement terms and a sample broadcast log.  ENIL FY25 annual report, pp. 15–16    Radio City.  Its Bazaar campaign provides a precedent for promoting local businesses. Ask whether a Delhi showroom visit and response tracking are available today.  Radio City Bazaar    RED FM.  Its Delhi page provides a dedicated advertiser contact. This reduces the work of finding the sales team. Request the host, time slot and delivery evidence in writing.  Delhi advertiser contact    BIG FM.  Public host, podcast and video listings give the dealer digital formats to discuss. Ask which Delhi slots, usage rights and host visits are inc"}, {"title": "Market & competitors · The market is moving at different speeds", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#market", "text": " FICCI-EY estimates India’s 2025 M&E market at ₹2.78 trillion. Its March 2026 release reports radio down 7% to ₹23 billion, live events up 44%, and digital advertising up 26%. These are calendar-year industry estimates; they are not Mirchi growth rates.   S9    Inference: diversification is commercially sensible, but an attractive category does not make every entrant or format profitable. Mirchi should choose businesses where its relationships, IP and city execution create an advantage after extra cost. "}, {"title": "Market & competitors · Competition by customer decision", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#landscape", "text": "     Arena  Relevant alternatives  What they compete on  Mirchi question      Local advertising  Radio City, RED FM, BIG FM, Fever, regional stations, local publishers, outdoor and digital ads.   S29   S30    Local reach, price, targeting, creative, delivery and evidence.  Why should a local advertiser renew this package?    Audio habit  Spotify, YouTube / YouTube Music, JioSaavn, Apple Music, Amazon Music and other audio services.  Catalogue, discovery, devices, reliability, price and convenience.  Which listening job can Gaana serve especially well?    Creator attention  YouTube, Instagram and independent regional creators.  Creator affinity, entertainment, distribution and community.  Can the creator relationship lead to an owned, useful service?    Going out  Concert promoters, local organisers, BookMyShow and District discovery.   S26   S27    Relevant supply, trust, availability, convenience and price.  Where does Mirchi add distinctive IP or execution value?    Brand solutions  Agencies, creator networks and platforms.  Strategy, creative quality, speed, measurement and commer"}, {"title": "Market & competitors · What others offer, and what Mirchi offers in return", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#comparison", "text": "     Competitor  What they offer that Mirchi could learn from  What Mirchi or Gaana brings  What to test      Radio City and BIG FM  Both compete for local attention. BIG FM puts RJs, podcasts and videos in the main navigation.   S29   S30    Mirchi can connect regional stories, local sales teams, Gaana and physical events.   S1   S14    Test whether a city and RJ directory helps people find their intended host or show faster than the editorial landing page.    RED FM and Fever  Alternative local stations in several Mirchi markets. Check programming and ad packages city by city.   S35    Mirchi can offer advertisers campaigns across scheduled radio ads, creators, events and digital services.   S7    Compare actual prices, audiences, delivery and renewal for the same brief in the same city.    Spotify India  Dedicated student plan and a Platinum tier with lossless audio. No equivalent Gaana plan was confirmed on the public price page reviewed.   S37   S23    Gaana showed cheaper standard web pricing in this snapshot, a one-time annual option, and Mirchi originals such as Sunday Suspen"}, {"title": "Market & competitors · Revenue, audience and engagement numbers", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#numbers", "text": "     Measure  Mirchi / Gaana  Benchmark  How to read it      Quarterly operating revenue  ENIL group ₹116.70 crore, down 17.3%.   S1    Music Broadcast ₹40.96 crore, up 8.2%, standalone.   S36    Same quarter ending September 2026. Scope and revenue mix differ, so this compares company direction and scale only.    Music audience  ENIL deck reports 20M Gaana monthly active users.   S1    Spotify reports 788M monthly active users and 306M Premium subscribers worldwide.   S38    Global Spotify is a scale reference. Gaana monthly users cannot reveal how many pay.    Listening activity  ENIL reports 30+ minutes per listening user in early Vivo engagement.   S1    No matching partner, population and time window verified.  Compare time per listening day, successful plays and repeat listening over 7 and 30 days within the same group.    India web price  Gaana ₹129 monthly, ₹709 yearly offer, ₹899 one-time annual.   S23    Spotify Standard ₹139 per month or ₹1,390 for one year. Student ₹69 per month.   S37    Public price snapshot. Check current offer and eligibility. These figures do not sho"}, {"title": "Market & competitors · Which gaps I would investigate first", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#next-moves", "text": "  Start with three real tasks: find a favourite RJ or show, play a regional episode from a social link, and book a local event. Compare steps and completion on desktop, phone browsers and installed apps.  Compare radio advertisers in the same city, category and time of day. Track actual price per ad second, percentage of ad time sold, repeat booking and profit after delivery costs.  Compare streaming groups by how they joined. Track first play, return after 7 and 30 days, paid renewal, refunds and profit per customer.  Compare repeat event formats by city, capacity and sponsor structure. Track booking completion, paid attendance, refunds, sponsor renewal and cash received before committing to costs.   A public feature gap is a reason to test a customer task. Missing public numbers are listed as questions for the operating team and do not count against either company. "}, {"title": "Market & competitors · A defensible position to test", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#position", "text": "   Analysis  Win locally  Connect an advertiser’s city objective with a credible host, useful local content and a relevant experience. Make delivery and response visible.    Analysis  Win through culture  Use regional voices and recognisable franchises to create a reason to return. Avoid a feature race with every large audio platform.    Analysis  Win through execution  A consistent operational experience can make a format easier for sponsors and attendees to repeat. Build repeatability around margin and service quality.   "}, {"title": "Market & competitors · SWOT, tied to decisions", "url": "/radio-mirchi/competition#swot", "text": "     Lens  Assessment  Decision implication      Strengths  Brand, cultural relevance, local talent, advertiser relationships, content and execution capabilities.  Build around existing advantages rather than unrelated software products.    Weaknesses to investigate  Cross-platform continuity, variable economics by business, execution complexity.  Measure before calling them proven failures.    Opportunities  Better conversion of existing reach, retained paid user groups, repeat sponsor formats.  Pilot focused improvements with profit after direct costs as the success measure.    Threats  Ad-budget shifts, creator/platform dependence, rights costs, event competition and demand volatility.  Use scenario planning, diversified channels and explicit downside limits.     "}, {"title": "Sources & research limits · What the additional reading contributes", "url": "/radio-mirchi/sources#digital-reading", "text": " The  ENIL media archive  documents the earlier FM expansion.  Fortune India  describes changing access to FM receivers and advertiser demand for measurable outcomes. Both help explain why a broadcaster would invest in digital distribution.   Business Today on Gen Z  supports testing whether younger buyers understand the value and trust an offer. Willingness to pay for a Mirchi show still requires a listening and pricing experiment. The  competitive comparison  links the survey, India price check and Spotify disclosures. The shared LinkedIn post and job listing could not be read directly. The role scope follows the supplied materials. "}, {"title": "Sources & research limits · Research method and confidence", "url": "/radio-mirchi/sources#method", "text": " Research date:  21 November 2026, India time . Financial cut-off: latest available Q2 / H1 FY27 disclosures. Source hierarchy: filed financial tables for scoped figures; management disclosures for strategy; official product pages for current propositions; public observations for experience; independent industry research for market context.      Label  Meaning  Limit      Reported  Company / official source says it.  Not independently audited unless explicitly stated.    Observed  A public browser surface was inspected in this session.  Session, region, viewport and eligibility may affect behaviour.    Listing evidence  Store description, release notes, ratings or screenshots.  Does not establish installed-app behaviour.    Hypothesis / proposed  Original analysis or a potential test.  Not a confirmed defect or promised business result.      Public browsing used a signed-out in-app browser. Desktop default was 1280 × 720. Mobile responsive checks used a 390 × 844 viewport; these are browser emulation, not a physical handset or native app test. No subscription or ticket purchase was c"}, {"title": "Sources & research limits · Reconciliation and evidence cautions", "url": "/radio-mirchi/sources#cautions", "text": "  Q2 EBITDA: domestic ₹11.26 crore / +9.5%; consolidated ₹12.05 crore / +5.9%. Different scopes explain different headlines.  March cash: consolidated approximately ₹423.9 crore, standalone ₹404.2 crore, distinguished in Q4 deck.  The Q2 deck executive summary prints digital revenue as ₹33 million, inconsistent with its 29% share. The press release gives ₹33 crore; the domestic minus existing business bridge is approximately ₹32.93 crore. Use that cross-check.  Annual narrative mix percentages and “digital as % of core radio” use different or unclear denominators. They are not combined into a precise annual pie chart.  The company profile’s 38M listener claim uses IRS 2019. Latest deck reach / event-count claims vary across slides; do not add them or invent a unique audience count.  Mirchi Plus store cache and live ratings differ. The live observed rating is recorded with its date rather than presented as a universal value.  Featured reviews include 2019-2023 and earlier years. They are not used to claim current defect prevalence or recent sentiment.  Mirchi iOS India URL was unavail"}, {"title": "Sources & research limits · Source register", "url": "/radio-mirchi/sources#registry", "text": " Find a source       S1 · 3-4 Nov 2026   ENIL Q2 FY27 investor presentation   Financial tables: PDF pp. 15-17. Initiatives: pp. 19-31. Company presentation; audience claims are not independently audited.  https://www.enil.co.in/pdf/stock_exchange_fillings/FY27/ENILInvestorsPresentationQ2FY27.pdf    S2 · 3 Nov 2026   ENIL Q2 FY27 results press release   Quarter ended 30 Sep 2026. Rounded revenue, domestic mix, digital losses and consolidated cash.  https://www.enil.co.in/pdf/stock_exchange_fillings/FY27/ENILPressReleaseQ2FY27.pdf    S3 · 4 Nov; filed 6 Nov 2026   ENIL Q2 FY27 earnings call transcript   Management commentary, not a forecast: radio pressure, event margin discipline, acquisition costs and Gaana ambition.  https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ENIL_06112026152711_ENIL20261106TranscriptQ2FY27.pdf    S4 · 18 May 2026   ENIL Q4 FY26 investor presentation   FY26 consolidated table: PDF p. 19. Product updates pp. 21-25; event cases pp. 26-29.  https://www.enil.co.in/pdf/stock_exchange_fillings/FY26/2026_05_18_Investor_PPT_Q4FY26.pdf    S5 · FY ended 31 Mar 2026   ENIL FY2"}, {"title": "Campaign prototype · How long does it take to recover the cost of a new subscriber?", "url": "/radio-mirchi/prototype#digital", "text": " Suppose a city show costs ₹30,000 to promote. Each active paying listener contributes ₹25 per month after variable costs. Choose a result to see how customer acquisition changes the decision. These are illustrative inputs.   150 extra payers  ₹30,000 ÷ 150 = ₹200 per payer. Recovering this takes eight paid months. If the typical payer leaves after four months, the campaign falls short by ₹100 per payer.    300 extra payers  ₹100 per payer takes four paid months to recover. A listener who leaves after two paid months contributes ₹50. Check the actual renewal distribution before expanding.    600 extra payers  ₹50 per payer takes two paid months. Check that the additional payers were caused by the campaign and that discounts did not reduce the ₹25 monthly contribution.    Change the assumptions in the financial calculator  "}, {"title": "Campaign prototype · What the dealer bought", "url": "/radio-mirchi/prototype#delivery", "text": "  Objective:  encourage weekend test drives. The example booking covers 30 radio ads and one host appearance. The station log confirms 28 ads and the appearance. Two ads remain due. Agree replacement slots with the dealer. "}, {"title": "Campaign prototype · What the dealer can measure", "url": "/radio-mirchi/prototype#response", "text": " The dealer recorded 18 test drives during the campaign weekend. Eight participants used the campaign booking code. Code use shows a recorded response. A comparable weekend or a controlled test would help estimate additional visits caused by the campaign. "}, {"title": "Campaign prototype · Compare a renewal offer", "url": "/radio-mirchi/prototype#offer", "text": " Choose the proposed terms    Standard renewal  Discount and extra host time     All amounts and counts here are examples. The dealer sees delivery and response. The station uses the renewal calculation internally.  Read the pilot design "}, {"title": "Campaign prototype · Listener product decision", "url": "/radio-mirchi/prototype#listener", "text": " Proposed pilot for one local show. Choose the observed result to see the next product decision. These are hypothetical outcomes.   People open the host’s link but rarely start listening  Check that the link opens the promised episode, the play control works on mobile web and the audio is available in that region. Compare successful plays per eligible visit before buying more traffic.    People listen once but seldom return  Test a visible next episode or a voluntary show reminder. Compare seven-day return among first-time listeners with the current experience, and monitor opt-outs.    Listeners return and a sponsor wants more ad time  Test one placement. Compare net sponsor income per eligible listener, completion and repeat listening against the current ad load. Expand only if the extra income covers production and the loss of future listening.  "}];
